# Keep Claude Code shell hooks LF-only so they run under Git Bash on Windows.
# core.autocrlf=true would otherwise check them out with CRLF, which breaks the
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash` shebang ("bad interpreter: ...^M") and the hook silently fails.
.claude/hooks/*.sh text eol=lf

# Keep the design-sync SOURCE surface LF-only. These files are inputs to the
# design-system bundle generator, and its `_ds_sync.json` verification anchor hashes the
# generated artifacts BYTE-wise. A session AUTHORS these with LF; core.autocrlf=true then
# re-materializes them as CRLF on the next checkout, so the very same content hashes
# differently and the driver reports the component as "changed" forever — phantom drift
# that erodes the diff signal real regressions rely on (proven 2026-07-24: the three Skel*
# components' prompt.md hashed cec8acd63db6 as CRLF vs the live c2910b870625 as LF, with
# byte-identical text). Pinning eol=lf makes every checkout match what the tools author.
.design-sync/**/*.md text eol=lf
.design-sync/**/*.ts text eol=lf
.design-sync/**/*.tsx text eol=lf
.design-sync/**/*.json text eol=lf

# The RETAINED OFFICIAL SOURCES are byte-pinned evidence, not text. `official-sources/manifest.json`
# records a SHA-256 per file and `registry_integrity` / `verify_repository_privacy` verify the bytes
# against it, so ANY line-ending translation invalidates the corpus. Caught at the staging gate on
# 2026-08-01: the IANA CSV is 1,156,064 bytes with 14,530 CRLF pairs in the worktree (hash matches the
# manifest) and normalized to 1,141,534 bytes / 0 CRLF in the INDEX — exactly one byte lost per CRLF —
# so `verify_repository_privacy.py` reported "indexed official source fails manifest integrity" for
# four files. Committing them normalized would ship a corpus that fails its own integrity check, and
# `distribution_verify.py` REQUIRES these files, so a clean-clone build would verify red.
#
# `-text` (not `eol=lf`) is deliberate: eol=lf would still be a translation, and these files must be
# stored EXACTLY as their publisher issued them, whatever their line endings are. Same reasoning as
# the .design-sync block above, which pins eol=lf because a byte-wise hash anchor cannot tolerate
# re-materialization — here the pinned bytes happen to be CRLF, so the only safe rule is "do not
# touch". Never "fix" trailing whitespace in these files either; it is publisher data and is hashed.
reference-data/official-sources/** -text
# The installed-wheel/Atlas EoL fixture is an exact byte-for-byte copy of the
# retained source above and is checked against the same SHA-256 at runtime.
cisco_toolkit/data/eol-bulletins.json -text

# The built AssessHub SPA is TRACKED so the wheel's bytes can be bound to a commit (without it,
# `distribution_verify --require-source-binding` exits 3 when shipped SPA members are outside the
# claimed commit. The Vite 8 bundle is index.html plus five content-hashed assets. Tracking
# it only works if the bytes survive round-tripping EXACTLY, because CI rebuilds the bundle and
# byte-compares it against these blobs: any line-ending translation makes a correct rebuild look like
# a modified tracked file and fails the immutability proof. Same rule and same reason as the retained
# official sources above — the reference-data corpus was caught by this at the staging gate on
# 2026-08-01 (one byte lost per CRLF against a SHA-256 manifest), so it is applied here BEFORE the
# first commit rather than after the first failure.
#
# Verified byte-reproducible on this platform before tracking: two consecutive `npm run build` runs
# produced an identical file set with identical SHA-256s for all six members. CROSS-PLATFORM
# reproduction (CI is ubuntu/node-24.19.0, these bytes were built on Windows) is NOT yet demonstrated —
# it is exactly what the CI step now proves or disproves on its first run.
# Rolldown preserves intentional trailing spaces inside dependency shader/template strings; those
# generated bytes are reproducibility inputs, not authored-source whitespace defects.
webapp/frontend/dist/** -text -whitespace
# ...and the HTML TEMPLATE the bundle is built FROM must materialize identically on every platform,
# because Vite passes the template's line endings straight through into dist/index.html. Proven by
# CI's original Vite 6 proof: all four content-hashed JS/CSS assets (1.8 MB of minified output)
# reproduced BYTE-IDENTICALLY from a Windows-built commit on ubuntu/node-20 — the only
# divergent file was index.html, 12 CRLF pairs from this template's autocrlf materialization vs
# ubuntu's pure-LF checkout. Minified code is EOL-insensitive; a verbatim template passthrough is not.
webapp/frontend/index.html text eol=lf

# Cloudflare parses this tracked deployment contract byte-for-byte. Pin LF so
# Windows and Linux builds feed the same header rules into the Sites archive.
master-reference/public/_headers text eol=lf

# The bounded image parser is a byte-bound local package. Its SBOM source
# receipt is computed over selected Git blobs, so every checkout must
# materialize the same LF bytes for local verification and package tests.
master-reference/vendor/bounded-image-size/** text eol=lf

# Local source archives are allowlisted for product source, not engagement or
# operator-state material. This does not rewrite Git history; it prevents
# accidental inclusion by `git archive` while the tracked corpus is sanitized.
/.claude export-ignore
/.design-sync export-ignore
/.gstack export-ignore
/docs export-ignore
/research_lane export-ignore
/tests export-ignore
/webapp/tests export-ignore
/AI_SESSION_CONTEXT.md export-ignore
/CHAT_SUMMARY.md export-ignore
/requirements.reference.json export-ignore
/devices.json export-ignore
/compass_artifact_* export-ignore
/scratch_* export-ignore
